      "score": 2,
      "rationale": "c7 establishes modest repository interest and forks, but does not establish independent integrations, retained users or paying usage; the supported portion of c8 concerns development-only signing."
    },
    "narrative": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The supported portions of c4 and c5 place the gateway clearly within agent interfaces and direct merchant payments, but do not establish a differentiated commercial wedge or credible platform expansion."
    },
    "execution": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "c10 establishes substantial recent owner-level activity, while supported portions of c1, c11 and c12 establish contributor activity, a recent repository push and CLI tooling; sustained gateway-specific shipping and rapid standards follow-up remain unproven."
    }
  },
  "recommendation": "pass",
  "missing_risks": [
    "Who would pay, for which recurring problem, and through what monetization model is unknown.",
    "Independent deployments, repeat usage, retention and production transaction activity are unverified.",
    "Differentiation from direct protocol integration and competing gateways is unproven; facilitator concentration does not establish gateway competition.",
    "Production security, authorization correctness, settlement recovery and independent audit coverage are unknown.",
    "Compatibility maintenance across experimental protocols could create substantial support costs.",
    "Gateway-specific ownership, commercialization commitment and allocation of engineering resources are unknown."
  ],
  "strongest_counterargument": "The evidence establishes an actively developed technical artifact and modest repository interest, but does not establish recurring demand or willingness to pay. A founder call has weak investment priority until independent production adoption and a credible commercial wedge are demonstrated."
